#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965ed6b0-0351-5fca-882d-0be68d74d9a6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Floor Function in More Complex Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "2a557e49-5a6c-56c1-9eb8-31e4690b08c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13202" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x) = \\dfrac{e^{\\left\\lfloor x+2 \\right\\rfloor}}{2x+1}$ at $x = 0.75$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is less than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 0.75$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.75) &= (e^{⌊0.75 + 2⌋})/(2(0.75) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\lfloor 2.75\\right\\rfloor = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.75) &= \\frac{e^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{2}}{2.5} \\\\\n&= 2.95562… \\\\\n&\\approx 2.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23654a6-05a8-5f59-84a7-2d7a2f758cb1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Ceiling Function in More Complex Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "44ea15c4-a9d9-529c-ac14-84ba39087e69" :question/math-academy-id "e-13203" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x) = \\left \\lceil e^{6x-5} \\right \\rceil$ at $x = 1.25$." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 1.25$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1.25) &= ⌈e^{6(1.25) - 5}⌉ \\\\\n&= ⌈e^{7.5 - 5}⌉ \\\\\n&= ⌈e^{2.5}⌉\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left \\lceil e^{2.5} \\right \\rceil = \\left \\lceil 12.182\\,49 \\ldots \\right \\rceil = 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1.25) &= ⌈e^{2.5}⌉ \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eacf715f-6da8-5173-af22-bce603dc83b8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Floor Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7fbd1a8c-bdd6-5d03-9b3a-69ed01962b1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6017" :question/problem "If $f(x)=2x+\\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{x}{5} \\right\\rfloor$, then what is the value of $f(-0.5)$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is smaller than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=-0.5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-0.5) &= 2 \\cdot (-0.5) + ⌊\\frac{-0.5}{5}⌋ \\\\\n&=-1 + ⌊ - 0.1⌋\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function we have\n\n$$\n⌊ - 0.1⌋ =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-0.5) &= -1 + ⌊ - 0.1⌋ \\\\\n&=-1 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45647b2d-10ce-521f-8601-123c659e15f5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Ceiling Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9f99283e-05ca-5dd5-97dd-3f872d84f38e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6022" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)=(\\lceil 6x-3 \\rceil -2)^3$ at $x=0.4$." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=0.4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.4) &= (⌈6(0.4) - 3⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (⌈2.4 - 3⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (⌈ - 0.6⌉ - 2)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\lceil -0.6 \\rceil = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.4) &= (⌈ - 0.6⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (0 - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965ed6b0-0351-5fca-882d-0be68d74d9a6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Floor Function in More Complex Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd55f5f8-35be-52f0-8d3c-4646b1a4c78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061338} {:answer/id #uuid "cf707768-3371-5273-b9f1-bece29eabef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94b281-5e61-590f-a5f2-80142eeaf8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061340} {:answer/id #uuid "191d474f-db13-5ecf-a170-9415a33af674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061341} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4d756c-23bd-5c6b-9d61-1877dc0c5ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf707768-3371-5273-b9f1-bece29eabef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061339} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac1da660-38bb-5d17-a2f6-55d6ba10a58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061337}] :question/id #uuid "8ab2d9f9-4d40-5fad-9c33-9145c56d8fdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-182107" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x) = ⌊\\ln (x + 2)⌋$ at $x = 11$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
